<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#taille">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taille</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The taille was a direct land tax on the peasantry and other non-nobles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-estate">Third Estate</a> in Ancien Régime <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, levied on each household according to the land it held and paid directly to the state. At first an exceptional tax raised in times of need, it became permanent in 1439, when Charles VII was granted the right to tax for a standing army during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>. Clergy, nobles, crown officers, soldiers, magistrates and towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> were exempt. Roughly half of royal income in the 1570s, it rose from 2.5 million livres in 1515 to a record 21 million in 1589, and became one of the most hated taxes of the Ancien Régime.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taille.jpg" alt="Taille" width="330" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rôle_de_la_Taille_à_Paris,_-_Archives_Nationales_-_AE-II-302.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Hundred Years' War gave the crown permanent taxes, above all the <b>taille</b> and the salt tax, the gabelle, levied without the estates' consent from the 1440s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>On their estates nobles held courts and police powers over their peasants and collected rents, dues and labour services, and in France they were exempt from the <b>taille</b>, the main direct tax.</blockquote><a href="/government/aristocracy">Aristocracy · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote>Privilege, not wealth alone, defined rank: the clergy and the nobles paid no <b>taille</b>, the chief direct tax, and only nobles could wear a sword.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancien-regime-france">Social Hierarchy of Ancien Régime France</a></li></ul></section>
